b45ab2ea7dfded3e835c49a79178e11d7dd06bb13760874bbdb.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}." :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-3x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/ed058963b391a4cdc66b2ab48483906a4d07759681c1f24e47ffc9e10775895f.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/6c92028370c86431ddc6ccd9513c273054d98b5add159e1bddf5a2e11751d987.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333034/field-1" "q-333034/field-2" "q-333034/field-3" "q-333034/field-4" "q-333034/field-5" "q-333034/field-6" "q-333034/field-7" "q-333034/field-8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333034"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333141/field-1/ac225e037427ea7fdc6f7e879ac709452f853ca3432bb046292aaaf20fbd5565" :answer/id #uuid "9e04d3f3-a1bf-560b-9474-e660de673faa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333141/field-1/a1ebd2482f5be58f7e4d544dab368c68ef21eb68b1b06c10c7f2bdd815b52cfb" :answer/id #uuid "c7522b34-3cc2-50d9-ac72-3028e9fba9af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333141/field-1/209fa84188f39ea7282b4bcf63e5012b63b27540484e1e7aa39b053622908f34" :answer/id #uuid "8d11edae-ad17-5da0-aa94-1464d0aad159" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333141/field-1/0b4fc2c65192b2cc173c4e8ad58547377955072334e