:db/id "q-333503/field-11/1b7d85263a0d06934f9f896b63b89e343630a1e91b99da775559942d35804e2c" :answer/id #uuid "6882bd47-99af-582f-b4b7-70e013a211f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333503/field-11" :answer-field/id #uuid "cde67e88-c5bf-5a12-a0e2-ebb072ac32c7" :answer-field/key "field-11" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333503/field-11/96e42420406ce3d5c9cf202ad544ab6a7bbeba66db8a326db261b3bc566b29a3" :answer-field/choices ["q-333503/field-11/cb2748aca1c175694eed95d4e07947a1de83d69ab0f20fd05bd4c7de7a0eb195" "q-333503/field-11/96e42420406ce3d5c9cf202ad544ab6a7bbeba66db8a326db261b3bc566b29a3" "q-333503/field-11/1b7d85263a0d06934f9f896b63b89e343630a1e91b99da775559942d35804e2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333503" :question/id #uuid "11de4dfc-3785-5eae-bf84-88a291c168b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-333503" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/e5c236defa66a00f12cd338ed0012cf91e4d7c15aa4865fc2b1edf5b0e2ea1da.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/97f7eb1fbddcd9d927447978efd4c42e705d6835bf0cc74525d42ded8c35346b.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/edc697ab38ae9a56d46181e0a45982b7045224de0cc3c4a786ddbc515f22196c.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333503/field-1" "q-333503/field-2" "q-333503/field-3" "q-333503/field-4" "q-333503/field-5" "q-333503/field-6" "q-333503/field-7" "q-333503/field-8" "q-333503/field-9" "q-333503/field-10" "q-333503/field-11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333503"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
